course: history
lesson: 41
title: Kyiv's beginnings and Kyivan Rus
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Danylo, a boy in the class, tells history like a football fan: Kyiv is the oldest, the biggest and the best, and anyone who disagrees is an enemy. The history teacher sometimes holds old wording from Soviet books. The wise way is to check: sometimes Danylo is right, sometimes he stretches it, sometimes the teacher has to update his notes.
explain: Kyiv is very old: it was clearly an important town by the 900s, long before Moscow is first mentioned in 1147, but nobody knows its exact birthday, so the 1,500 years celebrated in 1982 is a chosen round date, like a party for a grandad who lost his birth certificate.
explain: A legend says three brothers, Kyi, Shchek and Khoryv, and their sister Lybid founded the city; it is a legend, like Romulus and Remus for Rome, but the river Lybid and two hills still carry their names.
explain: Kyivan Rus was a big medieval state centred on Kyiv from the 800s to the 1200s; its first rulers came from Scandinavian Vikings, it soon spoke the local Slavic language, and today Ukraine, Belarus and Russia all count it as part of their history, while its capital is in Ukraine.
fact: Kyiv celebrated 1,500 years in 1982; the date was chosen in Soviet times from legends and archaeology, and the exact founding year is not known.
fact: Kyiv was an important town by the 10th century, and Moscow is first mentioned in a chronicle in 1147.
fact: The legend of Kyi, Shchek, Khoryv and their sister Lybid was written down in the Primary Chronicle; the river Lybid and the hills Shchekavytsia and Khorevytsia carry their names, but nobody can prove they were real people.
fact: Long before Kyiv, around 4000–3500 BC, farmers of the Cucuteni–Trypillia culture built huge settlements in what is now Ukraine and Moldova, some of the biggest in Europe at that time; some may have housed over 10,000 people, but nobody knows what language they spoke, so they cannot be called Ukrainians.
fact: Kyivan Rus was a large medieval state from the 9th to the 13th century, centred on Kyiv, covering lands of today's Ukraine, Belarus and part of Russia; historians call it the shared ancestor of all three.
fact: The chronicle says Varangian (Scandinavian) leaders such as Oleh came to rule Kyiv in the 9th century; names like Oleh (Helgi) and Olha (Helga) come from Old Norse, and within a few generations the rulers spoke the local Slavic language.
fact: Princess Olha ruled Kyiv as regent (ruling for her young son) after her husband Ihor was killed in 945; she reformed tax collection and was the first ruler of Rus to be baptised; the story of her burning a town with pigeons is a chronicle legend.
fact: The Byzantine writer Leo the Deacon described Prince Sviatoslav with a shaved head and one lock of hair; he defeated the Khazars, lost his war with Byzantium (the Greek-speaking empire whose capital was Constantinople) and was killed by Pechenegs, horsemen of the steppe, in 972, about five centuries before the first Cossacks.
fact: In the 11th century Kyiv was one of Europe's largest cities, perhaps 40,000–50,000 people, but Constantinople, Baghdad, Cordoba and Chinese cities were far larger.
fact: The Primary Chronicle (the Tale of Bygone Years) was put together around 1113, probably at the Kyiv Caves Monastery; it mixes facts and legends, so historians check it against archaeology and foreign sources.
fact: The name Rus first meant the ruling group, then the lands around Kyiv, then the whole state; the names Russia and Ruthenia both grew from it, and people in Ukrainian lands called themselves Rusyny for centuries.
fact: Saint Sophia Cathedral in Kyiv was built in the 11th century under Yaroslav the Wise; it keeps original mosaics and frescoes and is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, though Europe has older churches.
exercise: Is the exact year Kyiv was founded known? | No, it is very old but the exact year is unknown | Yes, it was founded on 1 May 482, as the old chronicle says | Yes, it was founded in 1147 | Yes, it was founded in 1982
exercise: Which city is first mentioned in a chronicle in 1147? | Moscow | Kyiv on the Dnipro | Lviv in the west | Odesa on the sea
exercise: In the legend, who was Lybid? | The sister of Kyi, Shchek and Khoryv | The first queen of France, who came from Kyiv | A Viking who ruled Kyiv | The monk who wrote the chronicle
exercise: What still carries the names from the Kyi legend? | A river and two hills in Kyiv | Three old Viking ships kept in a museum | The three gates of Moscow | Three stars in the sky
exercise: What did the Trypillia people build around 4000–3500 BC? | Huge farming settlements | Stone pyramids and temples | Castles with high towers | Big ports for ships
exercise: Why can we not call the Trypillia people Ukrainians? | Nobody knows what language they spoke, and Ukrainians appeared much later | They lived in Egypt | They wrote only in Latin, so we cannot read what they said about themselves | They were Vikings from Sweden
exercise: Which modern countries count Kyivan Rus as part of their history? | Ukraine, Belarus and Russia | Ukraine, Poland, Hungary and Slovakia | Only Russia, and no one else | Only Ukraine, and no one else
exercise: Where did the first ruling family of Rus come from, according to the chronicle? | Scandinavia, the Varangians | Rome, the legions | Mongolia, the Golden Horde | Greece, the city of Athens
exercise: Which name of a Rus ruler comes from Old Norse? | Olha, from Helga | Volodymyr, from Latin | Taras, from Greek | Bohdan, from Arabic
exercise: What did Princess Olha change while she ruled? | How taxes were collected | The name of the Dnipro | The calendar of the whole world | The language of Byzantium
exercise: How did Prince Sviatoslav die in 972? | He was killed by Pechenegs | He died of old age in Rome | He drowned in the Black Sea | He was crowned by the Pope and retired
exercise: How big was Kyiv in the 11th century? | One of Europe's biggest cities, but not the world's biggest | The biggest city in the whole world, bigger than any city in China | A small village of a few huts | Bigger than Constantinople and Baghdad together
exercise: When was the Primary Chronicle put together? | Around 1113 | Around the year 482 | Around 1654, under Khmelnytsky | Around 1991, at independence
exercise: What did the name Rus grow into? | Several names, including Russia and Ruthenia | Only the name Ukraine, and never any other name | Only the name Sweden | Nothing, the name vanished
exercise: Which Kyiv building from the 11th century still keeps its original mosaics? | Saint Sophia Cathedral | The Kremlin's Cathedral in Moscow | Westminster Abbey in London | The Golden Horde palace
exercise: Who built Saint Sophia Cathedral in Kyiv? | Yaroslav the Wise | Princess Olha of Kyiv | Hetman Ivan Mazepa | Empress Catherine II
test: Danylo says Kyiv is 1,500 years old exactly, it's in the books. The teacher says Kyiv is «medieval, like London». Who's closer? | Each is half right: Kyiv is much older than Moscow, but the 1,500 date is a chosen round date | Danylo, the exact birthday is 482 | The teacher, Kyiv is no older than London's oldest church, which was built in the Middle Ages too | Neither, Kyiv was founded in 1982
test: teacher says the Kyi brothers story is «just a fairy tale», Danylo's upset. what's the wise take? | It is a legend, not proven fact, but it is part of how a people remembers itself | It is proven history, archaeologists found Kyi's grave | It is worthless and should be forgotten | It was made up in 1982 for a party
test: Danylo reckons Ukrainians built the biggest towns on Earth 6,000 years ago. What's true? | Trypillia farmers built some of Europe's biggest settlements, but nobody knows their language | He's right, they were Ukrainians who spoke Ukrainian and built bigger towns than Egypt ever had | Nothing was built there before 1900 | They were Roman towns
test: The teacher says Kyivan Rus was «the first Russian state» and Danylo says «it was Ukraine, Russia stole the name». What do historians say now? | It was the shared ancestor of Ukraine, Belarus and Russia, with its capital in Kyiv | It was only Russian | It was only Ukrainian, and Russia took its name and history much later by stealing them | It never existed
test: Danylo says Vikings never ruled Kyiv, it's a German lie. What's the fix? | The chronicle and Norse names show a Viking-born dynasty, but the people and culture were mostly Slavic | He's right, there were no Scandinavians in Rus | Kyiv was a Viking colony where everyone spoke Swedish | The Vikings ruled Kyiv until 1991
test: A cartoon shows Olha burning a town by tying fire to pigeons. Which is fairest? | That story is a chronicle legend; that she ruled and reformed taxes is history | It is filmed proof from 945 | Olha never existed | Women could not rule anywhere in those days, so the whole story of Olha is made up
test: Danylo says Sviatoslav was a Cossack 'cause of his haircut. What's wrong? | The haircut detail is real, but the Cossacks appeared about five centuries later | Nothing, Sviatoslav founded the Sich | Sviatoslav had long hair, so it is all wrong | Cossacks lived before Kyiv was founded
test: Danylo says Kyiv in 1050 was the biggest city in the world; the teacher calls it «a village». Who's right? | Both overstate: it was one of Europe's great cities, not the world's biggest | Danylo, nothing on Earth was bigger, not even Constantinople or any city in China | The teacher, it had a few huts | Both, it changed every week
test: Why can't you just say «it's in the chronicle, so it's true»? | Chronicles mix facts with legends and must be checked against other sources | Chronicles are always invented | Chronicles were written yesterday | Nobody can read old chronicles at all
test: Danylo goes «Rus always meant Ukraine and nothing else». What's closer? | The old name changed meaning and grew into several modern names | He's right, it never meant anything else in any century, not even once | Rus always meant Sweden | Rus was a type of fish
test: Danylo says Saint Sophia is the oldest church in Europe. What's the honest pride? | It is a wonderful 11th-century cathedral with original mosaics, though not Europe's oldest | It is the oldest church on Earth, older than any church in Rome, Jerusalem, Greece or Armenia | It was built in 1982 | It is a copy of a Moscow church
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the founding date of the city of Kyiv? | The city is of great antiquity, but its precise year of foundation is not established | The city was demonstrably founded in the year four hundred and eighty-two | The city was founded subsequent to Moscow | The city was founded in nineteen hundred and eighty-two
exam: Identify the modern states which regard Kyivan Rus as constituting part of their history. | Ukraine, Belarus and Russia | Ukraine, Poland and Lithuania | The Russian Federation alone | The kingdoms of Sweden and Norway
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the origin of the first ruling dynasty of Kyivan Rus. | It was of Scandinavian origin and subsequently adopted the Slavic language | It was of Roman origin and conducted all its affairs and its correspondence in Latin | It was of Mongol origin and spoke Mongolian at court | It was elected by the Trypillia people
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which historians verify the Primary Chronicle against other evidence. | It combines factual record with legend and was composed long after many events | It was composed in the twentieth century | It contradicts every known coin and building | It was written in a language no one can read
